{% extends "layouts/docs" %}

{% block content %}
	<p><code>for</code> renders its content once for each item in a collection. An <code>else</code> section renders when there are no items.</p>

	{% apply example(context: { posts: [{ title: "Hello" }, { title: "Second post" }] }) %}
		{% verbatim %}
{% for post in posts %}
	<h2>{{ loop.index }}. {{ post.title }}</h2>
{% else %}
	<p>No posts yet.</p>
{% endfor %}
		{% endverbatim %}
	{% endapply %}

	<p><code>for</code> works with arrays, hashes, sets, maps and other iterables. Looping over null is the same as looping over an empty array. Async iterables aren't supported, because Kiln doesn't wait for them: have your function collect the items into an array.</p>

	<h2>Keys and values</h2>

	<p>Give two names to get each key and value. For arrays, the key is the index, starting at 0.</p>

	{% apply example(context: { settings: { theme: "dark", lang: "en" } }) %}
		{% verbatim %}
{% for key, value in settings %}
	{{ key }}: {{ value }}
{% endfor %}
		{% endverbatim %}
	{% endapply %}

	<h2>The loop variable</h2>

	<p>Inside a loop, <code>loop</code> tells you where you are:</p>

	<table>
		<thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr><td><code>loop.index</code></td><td>Position, starting at 1</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>loop.index0</code></td><td>Position, starting at 0</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>loop.revIndex</code></td><td>Items left, ending at 1</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>loop.revIndex0</code></td><td>Items left, ending at 0</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>loop.first</code></td><td><code>true</code> on the first item</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>loop.last</code></td><td><code>true</code> on the last item</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>loop.length</code></td><td>Total number of items</td></tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>

	{% apply example(context: { tags: ["fast", "safe", "async"] }) %}
		{% verbatim %}
{% for tag in tags %}{{ tag }}{{ loop.last ? "." : ", " }}{% endfor %}
		{% endverbatim %}
	{% endapply %}

	<h2>Nested loops</h2>

	<p>In a nested loop, <code>loop</code> refers to the inner loop. To use the outer one, save it first:</p>

	{% apply highlight("kiln") %}
		{% verbatim %}
{% for row in rows %}
	{% set outer = loop %}
	{% for cell in row %}
		{{ outer.index }}.{{ loop.index }}
	{% endfor %}
{% endfor %}
		{% endverbatim %}
	{% endapply %}

	<p>Each pass through the loop has its own scope. To build up a value across passes, set the variable before the loop. See <a href="/docs/language/variables/#assigning-variables">Assigning variables</a>.</p>
{% endblock %}
